    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kubrat"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kubrat.jpg" alt="Kubrat" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kubrat">Kubrat</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giudjenov_Kubrat_and_his_sons_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Dimitar Atanasov Gyudjenov (1891 - 1979), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgars</a> were a Turkic-speaking steppe people who in the early 7th century formed the confederation known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-great-bulgaria">Old Great Bulgaria</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kubrat">Khan Kubrat</a>, north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>. After Kubrat's death, around the 660s, pressure from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazars</a> broke it up, and one of his sons, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asparuh-of-bulgaria">Asparuh</a>, led his followers west to the region of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> delta, known to Byzantine writers as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onglos">Onglos</a>. In 680 the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-iv">Constantine IV</a> led an army and fleet against them, but his forces were routed as they withdrew, and the Bulgars crossed the Danube into the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moesia">Moesia</a>. In 681 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> made peace and agreed to pay an annual tribute, in effect recognising an independent state on former imperial territory south of the Danube, a concession without precedent in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>. Asparuh's Bulgars settled among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> of the region, including the group known as the Seven Slavic Tribes and the Severi, who became partners and subjects in the new realm. The khan established his residence at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliska">Pliska</a> in the northeastern plain, and Bulgar and Slav elements coexisted under a Bulgar ruling class. Over three centuries the Bulgar minority was gradually assimilated, and by the 10th century the state's language and culture had become predominantly Slavic.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/constantinople"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/constantinople.jpg" alt="Constantinople" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Byzantine_Constantinople-en.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cplakidas, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The economy rested on farming and stockbreeding in the plains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moesia">Moesia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>, with horses and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> important to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgar</a> elite and grain to the Slav peasantry. Trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> was regulated by treaties, notably one concluded in 716 that fixed the frontier and set rules for merchants, and disputes over trade could lead to war, as in 894 when Byzantium moved the Bulgarian market from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a>. The early Bulgars worshipped the sky god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangra">Tangra</a>, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> kept their own pagan cults, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boris-i-of-bulgaria">Boris I</a> accepted baptism from Byzantium in 864 and imposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> on his people. Monasteries and churches spread across the land; the Great Basilica at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliska">Pliska</a> was among the largest churches in southeastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/preslav">Preslav</a>, the capital from 893, became known for its Round Church and painted ceramic tiles. In the 10th century, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a> Peter I, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogomil">priest Bogomil</a> founded a dualist religious movement, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogomilism">Bogomils</a>, which rejected the church hierarchy and the material world and later spread to Byzantium and western Europe. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cosmas-the-priest">Cosmas the Priest</a> wrote a treatise against them that also describes the social conditions of the time. Society was divided between a powerful landed aristocracy, the free peasantry and dependent labourers.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-anchialus-763"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-anchialus-763.jpg" alt="Battle of Anchialus (763)" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-anchialus-763">Battle of Anchialus (763)</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Solidus-Leo_III_and_Constantine_V-sb1504.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The early Bulgarian army combined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgar</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heavy-cavalry">heavy cavalry</a> with Slav infantry and depended on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fortified-camp">fortified camps</a> and long earthwork ramparts, including the great earthwork around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliska">Pliska</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erkesiya">Erkesiya</a> rampart facing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Byzantine Thrace</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tervel">Tervel</a> helped the deposed emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-ii">Justinian II</a> regain his throne in 705 and in 717–718 sent forces that assisted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> against the Arab siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. In the 8th century the emperor Constantine V waged repeated campaigns and won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-anchialus-763">Battle of Anchialus</a> in 763, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kardam">Kardam</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-vi">Constantine VI</a> at Markellai in 792. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krum">Krum</a> (c. 803–814) captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serdica">Serdica</a> in 809. When the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikephoros-i">Nikephoros I</a> sacked Pliska in 811, Krum trapped and destroyed his army in a mountain pass; the emperor was killed. Krum won again at Versinikia in 813 and threatened Constantinople. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simeon-i-of-bulgaria">Simeon I</a> (893–927) fought Byzantium and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyar</a> allies, calling in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pechenegs">Pechenegs</a>, who drove the Magyars west, and won at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-bulgarophygon">Bulgarophygon</a> in 896. His greatest victory came at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-achelous">Battle of Achelous</a> in 917, where a large Byzantine army was destroyed. Simeon conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> in 924 but was defeated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> in 926. Under Samuel the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-ii">Basil II</a> at the Gates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a> in 986, but were crushed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kleidion">Kleidion</a> in 1014.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/krum"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/krum.jpg" alt="Krum" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/krum">Krum</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Krum33.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Manasias, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The early rulers bore the title <em>kanasubigi</em>, recorded in Greek-language inscriptions, and governed with a council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgar</a> nobles, the boils and bagains, and high officials such as the <em>kavkhan</em> and the <em>ichirgu-boil</em>. Inscriptions carved on stone columns at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliska">Pliska</a> and on the cliff at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madara">Madara</a> record treaties, buildings and military events, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madara-rider">Madara Rider</a> relief, probably of the early 8th century, is the best-known monument of the pagan period. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krum">Krum</a> is said by a later Byzantine lexicon to have issued harsh laws against theft and drunkenness. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omurtag">Omurtag</a> (814–831) made a thirty-year peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> and built palaces and fortifications. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boris-i-of-bulgaria">Boris I</a>'s conversion was a political act: he crushed a revolt of pagan nobles in 866, negotiated with both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, and in 870 obtained an autonomous archbishopric under Constantinople. He reorganised the realm into provinces governed by royal appointees. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simeon-i-of-bulgaria">Simeon I</a>, educated in Constantinople, claimed the imperial title and was crowned by the Patriarch <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-i-mystikos">Nicholas Mystikos</a> in 913 in a ceremony whose meaning was later disputed. In 927 Byzantium recognised his son Peter I as tsar, married him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-lekapene">Maria Lekapene</a>, granddaughter of Romanos I, and accepted the Bulgarian church as a patriarchate.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/preslav"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/preslav.jpg" alt="Preslav" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/preslav">Preslav</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Preslav_fortress_11.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Izvora, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The First Bulgarian Empire's greatest legacy is Slavonic Christian literacy. When disciples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyril-and-methodius">Cyril and Methodius</a>, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clement-of-ohrid">Clement</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naum-of-ohrid">Naum</a>, were expelled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> in 885–886, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boris-i-of-bulgaria">Boris I</a> received them and gave them the means to teach. Clement worked at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohrid">Ohrid</a> in the southwest, while at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/preslav">Preslav</a> scholars developed the Cyrillic alphabet, based on Greek letters, alongside the older Glagolitic script created by Cyril. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simeon-i-of-bulgaria">Simeon I</a>'s reign brought a golden age of Slavonic letters: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-the-exarch">John the Exarch</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-of-preslav">Constantine of Preslav</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chernorizets-hrabar">Chernorizets Hrabar</a>, author of the treatise <em>On the Letters</em>, translated and composed theological, historical and scientific works. These texts and the Cyrillic script passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a> after its conversion in 988, and Old Church Slavonic became the liturgical and literary language of much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> Slavic world; Cyrillic is today used for Bulgarian, Serbian, Russian, Ukrainian and many other languages. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> also served as a model of a Christian Slavic monarchy modelled on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, with its own patriarch and tsar. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogomilism">Bogomil movement</a> influenced dualist heresies as far west as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catharism">Cathars</a> of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. The memory of the empire inspired the founders of the Second Bulgarian Empire in 1185 and modern Bulgarian national identity.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-dorostolon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/siege-of-dorostolon.jpg" alt="Siege of Dorostolon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-dorostolon">Siege of Dorostolon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BelagerungvonDorostolon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Henryk Siemiradzki, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The peaceful reign of Peter I (927–969) was followed by disaster. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> encouraged Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sviatoslav-i-of-kiev">Sviatoslav of Kiev</a> to attack <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> in 968, and he took much of the country, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/preslav">Preslav</a>, and planned to settle on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. The emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-i-tzimiskes">John I Tzimiskes</a> then intervened, captured Preslav in 971 and besieged Sviatoslav at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-dorostolon">Dorostolon</a> until he withdrew. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boris-ii-of-bulgaria">Boris II</a> was taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and stripped of his insignia, and eastern Bulgaria was annexed. In the west, the four sons of the noble Nicholas, known as the Cometopuli, rose in 976. The youngest, Samuel, emerged as ruler, defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-ii">Basil II</a> at the Gates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a> in 986 and, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-prespa">Prespa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohrid">Ohrid</a>, extended his power to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durres">Dyrrhachium</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a> and Duklja, taking the title of tsar around 997. From 1001 Basil II waged a long war of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attrition">attrition</a>, taking fortress after fortress. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kleidion">Battle of Kleidion</a> in July 1014 he destroyed Samuel's army; a later Byzantine chronicler claims he blinded about 15,000 prisoners, a number historians treat with caution. Samuel died that autumn. His successors <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gavril-radomir">Gavril Radomir</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-vladislav">Ivan Vladislav</a> could not stop Basil, and after Ivan Vladislav's death at Dyrrhachium in 1018 the Bulgarian nobility surrendered, and Bulgaria became a Byzantine province until 1185.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The First Bulgarian Empire was the first lasting independent state established on former imperial land in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>, a rival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> for three centuries, and the cradle of Slavonic Christian literature and the Cyrillic alphabet, which through its schools at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/preslav">Preslav</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohrid">Ohrid</a> spread across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> Slavic world.</em></p>

